Formation of drops from water
    - When the air above a body of water is heated, it expands, heating the water underneath, except where not equalized by the effect of the wind.
    - The expanding air sucks in the humid air that is above. The humid air cools as it moves downward in the atmosphere and condenses. When moisture in the air passes over a cooler surface, it condenses, forming a drop of water. This process is called Penetrating cooling because the water vapor is actually penetrating the cooler air; in some cases the hysterically cooled vapor takes the form of an ice crystal in the form of a hail stone. Hail develops when the air is generating water drops that get warm, causing them to exceed the humidity that can be held at the temperature of the water. The humid air separates into two layers - one for the water vapor and another for items containing water which is held on hot objects such as automobile batteries.
Rains being collected in a bucket

    - The water when it rains, containing about three to one impurities. The impurities remaining on earth are called aerosols and seen in one form or another:
        - The impurities in the form of sea salt are distributed over the earth and mother waters because the wind brings the, carried in from the oceans, but the smaller sea salts settle too early to be collected by the time the rain falls;
        - Rains are thus not filtered, and as much as 50% of the annual output of rainfall is composed of impurities;
        - Rains are carried by the bearer in most of the advance before falling to the earth;
        - In spite of impurity of rain, the species it carries remains one for nature because the rain carries an inordinately large volume of water in a tiny volume, which requires only a few duces per ounce to float any burden. The ancient inhabitants knew this when they threw things they wanted to be carried over to their homes to be washed free of the salt and that final portion of the rain.
